# EpiGraph Explorer — Caddy configuration.
#
# RECOMMENDED: a SITE BLOCK OF ITS OWN, so the Explorer has an origin to
# itself. Add the block below to the VM Caddyfile, alongside (not inside) the
# existing epigraph-api site block. A PATH MOUNT on a shared origin is kept at
# the bottom of this file as an alternative, with the trust-boundary cost it
# carries spelled out; read that before choosing it.
#
# Assumes the explorer listens on 127.0.0.1:8096 on the same host: either the
# systemd unit (epigraph-explorer.service.example) or a --network host
# container. The binary binds loopback only, so Caddy is the public edge.

explorer.example.com {
    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8096
}

# With:
#   EPIGRAPH_EXPLORER_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://explorer.example.com
#   EPIGRAPH_OAUTH_BASE_URL=https://api.example.com
# and the `oauth_clients` row's redirect_uris containing exactly
#   https://explorer.example.com/auth/callback
# (plain string match, byte for byte — no trailing slash).
#
# WHY A DEDICATED ORIGIN. The browser's security boundary is the ORIGIN
# (scheme + host + port), not the path. Everything the Explorer relies on to
# separate itself from other apps is origin-scoped, so co-hosting dissolves
# it:
#
#   * Cookies. `epx_session` is a bearer-equivalent credential and `epx_login`
#     binds a sign-in to a browser. A cookie `Path` is NOT a security
#     boundary: any page on the origin can read `document.cookie` (for a
#     non-HttpOnly cookie) and any page can SET a cookie at any path, so a
#     co-hosted app can write both of the Explorer's names.
#   * On an origin of its own with https, the Explorer names its cookies
#     `__Host-epx_session` and `__Host-epx_login` (Path=/, Secure, no Domain).
#     A browser honours a `__Host-` cookie only in exactly that shape, which
#     is what makes it unwritable from another host. This naming is automatic:
#     it switches on when the cookies are Secure AND the base path is the
#     root, and the process logs which pair it is using at startup.
#   * CSP `default-src 'self'` / `script-src 'self'` / `connect-src 'self'` /
#     `form-action 'self'`. `'self'` is the origin. On a shared origin the CSP
#     permits every script, fetch target and form action that any co-hosted
#     app serves.
#   * The `Origin` check on POST /auth/logout and POST /auth/redeem compares
#     the public ORIGIN, so a co-hosted page passes it.
#   * `Referrer-Policy: same-origin` sends the full URL of an Explorer page —
#     which can carry a claim id — to every other app on the origin.
#   * The postMessage `targetOrigin` for the Notion embed handoff code is the
#     origin, so any page on it can receive a single-use sign-in code.
#   * localStorage, sessionStorage, IndexedDB and the service-worker scope are
#     all per-origin.
#
# DO NOT deploy the Explorer under a wildcard-DNS parent domain that is not
# on the Public Suffix List (https://publicsuffix.org/). Cookies are not
# origin-scoped: under such a parent, any sibling host can `Set-Cookie` with
# `Domain=<parent>` and the victim's browser will send it here. The `__Host-`
# prefix above is the defence, so losing it (a base path, or
# EPIGRAPH_EXPLORER_INSECURE_COOKIES) and a non-PSL wildcard parent is the
# combination to avoid.
#
# /oauth/* MUST STAY BROWSER-REACHABLE ON THE API'S OWN ORIGIN. Sign-in is
# the API's own OAuth authorization server, and the browser navigates to it
# directly:
#   GET  /oauth/authorize            (explorer -> API, with PKCE)
#   GET  /oauth/callback             (Google -> API)
#   POST /oauth/authorize/consent    (the consent form's root-relative action)
# These live at the root of the API's EPIGRAPH_PUBLIC_BASE_URL and are
# already exposed for the claude.ai connector. Do not move them behind the
# Explorer, and do not let a matcher here shadow them. Point
# EPIGRAPH_OAUTH_BASE_URL at that browser-facing API origin. Only /api/v1 can
# stay hidden behind the Explorer (it reaches the API on loopback via
# EPIGRAPH_API_URL). The Explorer's own sign-in routes are /auth/*, which are
# distinct from the API's /oauth/*.
#
# RATE LIMITING /auth/login. Every anonymous GET of that path stores a
# pending login in the explorer's process-local map (10-minute TTL, 10 000
# entries). The explorer keeps the map bounded by evicting its oldest entries
# rather than refusing, so a flood can no longer lock anyone out of sign-in —
# but it can still evict other people's in-flight attempts, which makes them
# retry. An edge rate limit on that one path removes even that. Caddy has no
# rate limiter in the standard build; with the `caddy-ratelimit` module built
# in, add inside the site block:
#
#   @login path /auth/login
#   rate_limit @login {
#       zone explorer_login {
#           key    {remote_host}
#           events 30
#           window 1m
#       }
#   }
#
# Do not rate-limit `/auth/callback`: one slow sign-in behind a shared NAT
# would then fail at the last step.
#
# HEADERS. The explorer sets its own Content-Security-Policy, including
# `frame-ancestors` (the Notion embed depends on it), X-Content-Type-Options,
# Referrer-Policy and Cache-Control. Do not add X-Frame-Options or a second
# CSP in Caddy: a site-wide `X-Frame-Options: DENY` breaks the embed. Do not
# strip the Origin request header (Caddy forwards it by default): POST
# /auth/logout and POST /auth/redeem compare it with the public origin. Do
# not add caching for this site: the Explorer marks its own pages
# `private, no-store` and its static assets as cacheable.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ALTERNATIVE (NOT RECOMMENDED): a path on a shared origin.
#
# Only if a second hostname and certificate are genuinely unavailable. Append
# this inside the public site block of the VM Caddyfile, next to the existing
# epigraph-api stanzas (same pattern as services/nli):
#
#   handle_path /explorer* {
#       reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8096
#   }
#
# Understand what you are accepting: EVERY APP ON THAT ORIGIN IS INSIDE THE
# EXPLORER'S TRUST BOUNDARY. For each bullet under "WHY A DEDICATED ORIGIN"
# above, a co-hosted app — including one someone else deploys there later —
# can do the thing that bullet describes: read or overwrite the session and
# binding cookies, be loaded as script by the Explorer's own CSP, post to
# /auth/logout and /auth/redeem past the Origin check, and receive the
# postMessage sign-in handoff. A compromise of any app on the origin is a
# compromise of every Explorer session. The `__Host-` cookie prefix is
# unavailable on this layout, because it requires Path=/.
#
# PREFIX STRIPPING. `handle_path` strips the matched `/explorer` prefix before
# proxying, so the explorer receives `/claim/<id>`, not
# `/explorer/claim/<id>`. It cannot recover the prefix from the request, so
# EPIGRAPH_EXPLORER_PUBLIC_BASE_URL must carry it:
#   EPIGRAPH_EXPLORER_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://api.example.com/explorer
# Every link, redirect, og:url, the cookie Path and the OAuth redirect_uri
# (`.../explorer/auth/callback`, which must match the oauth_clients row byte
# for byte) are generated from it. The explorer also answers on the
# unstripped paths, so `handle /explorer* { ... }` works too; pick one. The
# rate-limit matcher above becomes `/auth/login` because `handle_path` has
# already stripped the prefix.
